<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Book of Common Prayer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1549">1549</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Book of Common Prayer is the prayer book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>, first issued in 1549 under Edward VI and largely composed by Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>. It replaced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> services with a single English liturgy, revised in 1552, 1559 and 1662. The 1662 version remained the official liturgy for centuries, and its language and forms spread throughout the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglican Communion</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer.jpg" alt="Book of Common Prayer" width="320" height="549" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Church of England · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Book_of_Common_Prayer_1760.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> the parish worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> followed the Latin Roman Rite, in local forms called uses, of which the most common in southern England was that of Sarum (Salisbury). There was no single book: the services were spread through the Missal, the Breviary, the Manual and the Pontifical. After Edward VI succeeded in 1547, revision could move faster. Parliament passed the Act of Uniformity on 21 January 1549, and the new book had to be in use by Whitsunday, 9 June. Thomas Cranmer, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>, is credited with the overall editorship and structure, though he borrowed and adapted material from other sources.</p><p>The 1549 book was intended as a temporary expedient. The 1552 revision, from the same editorial hand, broke decisively with the past in the view of the historian Christopher Haigh: it rewrote the baptism, confirmation, communion and burial services, removed ceremonies Protestants disliked and dropped exorcism and anointing. It was used only for a few months before Mary I restored Roman Catholic worship in 1553.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The 1549 book was the first prayer book to give complete forms of service for daily and Sunday worship in English. It contains Morning and Evening Prayer, the Litany, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Holy Communion</a> and occasional services in full, including orders for baptism, confirmation, marriage, prayers for the sick and a funeral service. It sets out the collects and the epistle and gospel readings for Sunday Communion, and tabulates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> readings, the Psalms and the canticles. With a calendar and lectionary, a priest needed only a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-psalms">Psalter</a> besides it. An ordinal for ordaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, priests and deacons was added in 1550.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>'s first Parliament authorised the 1559 book, which reintroduced the 1552 text with modifications. The Act of Uniformity passed the House of Lords by only three votes, and all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> who could attend voted against it. James I ordered further changes after the Hampton Court Conference of 1604. After the English Civil War, when the book had been abolished, the 1662 edition was printed two years after the restoration of the monarchy; it remains the official prayer book of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglicanism">Anglicanism</a> spread with British colonial expansion, and versions of the book are used in over 50 countries and over 150 languages. It was first translated into Manx in 1610 and Irish in 1712, and into Māori in 1830. Lutheran, Methodist and Presbyterian prayer books have borrowed from it, and its marriage and burial rites passed into other denominations and into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a>. Like the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/king-james-version">King James Version</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> and the works of Shakespeare, it has given many phrases to everyday English.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Book of Common Prayer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church of England, created by the Reformation and given its liturgy in Thomas Cranmer’s <b>Book of Common Prayer</b>, became the mother church of the worldwide Anglican Communion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>New texts gave the churches their shape: Luther’s German Bible and his Small Catechism of 1529, the Augsburg Confession drawn up by Philip Melanchthon in 1530, Thomas Cranmer’s <b>Book of Common Prayer</b> of 1549 and Reformed statements of faith such as the Heidelberg Catechism of 1563.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>The Church of England kept it in its <b>Book of Common Prayer</b> of 1549, then moved it to the end of the communion service, where it stayed in the 1552 and 1662 books until the 20th century.</blockquote><a href="/songs/gloria-in-excelsis-deo">Gloria in excelsis Deo</a></li><li><blockquote>Because the New Version and its supplement were bound up with every copy of the <b>Book of Common Prayer</b>, the carol spread widely and became one of the &quot;great four&quot; Anglican hymns.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hark-the-herald-angels-sing">Hark! The Herald Angels Sing</a></li><li><blockquote>Since 1549 Anglicans have normally sung or said it in English; the <b>Book of Common Prayer</b> of 1552 set it into a recitation of the Ten Commandments, and modern revisions have restored the choice of saying it without them.</blockquote><a href="/songs/kyrie">Kyrie</a></li><li><blockquote>The traditional Anglican Evening Prayer did not call for it, but the American <b>Book of Common Prayer</b> of 1979 prescribes it as an optional canticle before the psalms, and the Lutheran Service Book of the Lutheran Church – Missouri Synod includes it in early evening prayer.</blockquote><a href="/songs/phos-hilaron">Phos Hilaron (O Gladsome Light)</a></li></ul></section>
